    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d479">

                <pb facs="0650" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg526"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3984</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Secretary of the
                        Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Denby</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-22"><hi rendition="#italic">August 22,
                        1921</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge, with
                    thanks, your letter of July 27, 1921, enclosing a copy of a report dated June
                    7th from the Commander-in-Chief of the United States Asiatic Fleet, at Shanghai,
                    China, with reference to the placing of armed guards on vessels operating on the
                    Yangtze River.</p>
                <p>In this connection, and with reference to previous letters on the same matter, I
                    am transmitting for your information and such action as the situation requires,
                    a copy of a despatch, No. 272 [<hi rendition="#italic">273</hi>] of June 24,
                    with enclosure, from the American Consulate General at Hankow,<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d479-f1"/> relative to conditions on the Upper Yangtze River. I have
                    the honor to invite your attention to the suggestion made in the paragraph which
                    begins at the bottom of page 5 of the enclosure to the despatch from the
                    Consulate General, namely, that naval convoys be provided for the protection of
                    American chartered junks through danger zones in the upper reaches of the
                    Yangtze river, and to ask whether it would be possible for the Yangtze patrol to
                    put such a suggestion into effect.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">F. M.
                            Dearing</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Assistant
                        Secretary</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
